.TH SNSLICE 1 "2026-09-08" "snslice 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
snslice \(em partition NDJSON and CSV streams on complete record boundaries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snslice
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ INPUT_FILE ]
.P
.B snslice \-\-help
.P
.B snslice \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snslice
is an intentionally small, auditable, dependency\-free ISO C17 command\-line
utility for Linux systems that partitions structured streaming datasets\(emspecifically
Newline\-Delimited JSON
.RB ( NDJSON )
and Comma\-Separated Values
.RB ( CSV )\(eminto
bounded, deterministically named chunk files strictly on complete record boundaries
without loading the entire input or whole chunks into memory.
.P
When
.I INPUT_FILE
is omitted or supplied as
.BR \- ,
.B snslice
reads from standard input
.RB ( stdin ).
.P
.B snslice
guarantees that records are never fractured across chunk files.
For NDJSON, logical records correspond to newline\-terminated lines.
For CSV, RFC 4180 multiline rules are enforced: newlines, commas, and escaped quotes
inside double\-quoted fields are treated as literal field data rather than record delimiters.
The utility operates with constant O(1) resident set size (RSS) memory, creates chunk files
atomically to prevent overwrites, and transactionally unlinks incomplete partial chunks
on operational failures.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bytes =\fIBYTES\fR
Chunk size threshold in bytes.
.I BYTES
must be a strictly positive integer in the range 1 to
.BR SIZE_MAX .
At least one threshold
.RB ( \-\-bytes
or
.BR \-\-records )
must be specified.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-records =\fICOUNT\fR
Chunk size threshold in record count.
.I COUNT
must be a strictly positive integer in the range 1 to
.BR SIZE_MAX .
At least one threshold
.RB ( \-\-bytes
or
.BR \-\-records )
must be specified.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format =\fIFORMAT\fR
Stream parsing format:
.B ndjson
(the default) or
.BR csv .
Any other value is rejected fail\-closed with exit status
.BR 2 .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-out\-dir =\fIDIR\fR
Destination directory where output chunk files are created.
Defaults to the current working directory
.RB ( . ).
The directory must exist and be accessible with write and search permissions;
otherwise
.B snslice
fails closed with exit status
.BR 2 .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix =\fIPREFIX\fR
Output chunk filename prefix.
Defaults to
.BR chunk_ .
The prefix must not be empty, must not contain directory separators
.RB ( / ),
and must not contain directory traversal tokens
.RB ( .. ).
Violations fail closed with exit status
.BR 2 .
.TP
.B \-\-
Option terminator.
All subsequent command\-line arguments are treated as the positional
.I INPUT_FILE
operand.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print command synopsis and option summary to standard output and exit with status
.BR 0 .
Must be supplied as the sole argument; combining
.B \-\-help
with any other option or operand fails closed with exit status
.BR 2 .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print canonical version information
.RB ( \(lq "snslice 0.1.0" \(rq )
to standard output and exit with status
.BR 0 .
Must be supplied as the sole argument; combining
.B \-\-version
with any other option or operand fails closed with exit status
.BR 2 .
.SH FORMATS
.B snslice
supports two structured streaming data formats:
.SS Newline-Delimited JSON (NDJSON)
.IP \(bu 3
Selected by
.B \-\-format ndjson
(the default).
.IP \(bu 3
Each line terminated by LF
.RB ( \en )
or CRLF
.RB ( \er\en )
is recognized as an atomic record.
.IP \(bu 3
Blank lines (lines containing only newline characters) are valid records in NDJSON
streams and are preserved without modification.
.IP \(bu 3
Embedded NUL bytes
.RB ( \e0 )
are strictly forbidden and immediately terminate execution fail\-closed with diagnostic
.B snslice: MALFORMED_NDJSON: embedded NUL byte detected
and exit status
.BR 2 .
.IP \(bu 3
If the input stream ends with an unterminated line (bytes accumulated without a trailing newline),
.B snslice
terminates fail\-closed with diagnostic
.B snslice: MALFORMED_NDJSON: unterminated record at EOF
and exit status
.BR 2 .
.SS Comma-Separated Values (CSV, RFC 4180)
.IP \(bu 3
Selected by
.BR "\-\-format csv" .
.IP \(bu 3
Fields may be enclosed in balanced double quotes
.RB ( \(dq ).
.IP \(bu 3
Embedded newlines
.RB ( \en
and
.BR \er\en ),
commas
.RB ( , ),
and escaped double quotes
.RB ( \(dq\(dq )
inside quoted fields are treated as literal field data.
They do not trigger field or record delimiters.
.IP \(bu 3
A true record boundary is recognized if and only if an unquoted LF
.RB ( \en )
is encountered outside of quoted fields.
.IP \(bu 3
Multiline CSV rows (such as database text exports, JSON payloads, or stack traces)
remain intact within a single chunk file and are never severed across chunks.
.IP \(bu 3
Embedded NUL bytes
.RB ( \e0 )
trigger immediate fail\-closed termination with diagnostic
.B snslice: MALFORMED_CSV: embedded NUL byte detected
and exit status
.BR 2 .
.IP \(bu 3
If the input stream reaches EOF while still inside a double\-quoted field or expecting
an escaped quote resolution, execution aborts fail\-closed with diagnostic
.B snslice: MALFORMED_CSV: unterminated quoted field at EOF
and exit status
.BR 2 .
.SH CHUNK-SIZE SEMANTICS
.IP \(bu 3
.B Threshold Requirement:
At least one chunk size threshold
.RB ( \-\-bytes
or
.BR \-\-records )
must be specified on the command line.
Omitting both triggers a fail\-closed usage error with exit status
.BR 2 .
.IP \(bu 3
.B Dual Threshold Evaluation:
If both
.B \-\-bytes
and
.B \-\-records
are specified, a chunk split occurs as soon as
.I either
threshold is reached or exceeded upon completing a record.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Record Boundary Invariant:
A record is
.B never
split across chunk files.
Slicing decisions take effect strictly at complete record boundaries.
If a single record exceeds the byte threshold, it is written in its entirety to
the active chunk, which is closed immediately after the record's terminating newline.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Empty Input Handling:
If the input stream contains 0 bytes (an empty file or empty standard input),
.B snslice
exits cleanly with status
.BR 0 ,
creates zero chunk files on disk, and leaves standard output and standard error empty.
.SH OUTPUT NAMING
.IP \(bu 3
Output chunk files are created in
.I DIR
specified by
.B \-\-out\-dir
(default:
.BR . )
with the filename pattern:
.RS 3
.P
.BI "<out-dir>/" "<prefix>" "%05zu." "<ext>"
.RE
.IP \(bu 3
The chunk counter
.RB ( %05zu )
is a 1\-indexed, five\-digit zero\-padded decimal integer starting at
.B 00001
and incrementing sequentially
.RB ( 00002 ,
.BR 00003 ,
\&...).
.IP \(bu 3
The filename extension
.RB ( <ext> )
is
.B ndjson
for NDJSON format and
.B csv
for CSV format.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Lazy Creation:
The first chunk file
.RB ( 00001 )
is opened lazily upon reading the first valid byte of input.
If the input stream contains zero bytes, no chunk files are created on disk.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Atomic Collision Defense:
Every chunk file is created using
.BR open (2)
with flags
.BR "O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_WRONLY"
and permission mode
.BR 0644 .
If a chunk file of the intended name already exists on disk,
.B snslice
immediately aborts fail\-closed with diagnostic
.BI "snslice: OUTPUT_COLLISION: \(dq" PATH "\(dq"
and exit status
.BR 2 .
Pre\-existing files are never overwritten, truncated, or appended to.
.SH FAILURE CLEANUP
.IP \(bu 3
.B Transactional Incomplete Chunk Removal:
If any operational error occurs during streaming\(emincluding write failures
.RB ( ENOSPC ,
disk full), read errors
.RB ( EIO ),
malformed stream syntax, record length violations, or memory allocation failures\(emthe
currently active, partially written chunk file is immediately closed and removed from
the filesystem via
.BR unlink (2)
before process termination.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Completed Chunk Preservation:
Previously completed and closed chunk files are preserved intact on disk; only
the corrupt partial active chunk is unlinked.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Descriptor Discipline:
At most two file descriptors are open concurrently: the input stream descriptor and
the single active output chunk descriptor.
When a chunk finishes, its descriptor is closed before the next chunk file is opened.
.SH RESOURCE BEHAVIOR
.IP \(bu 3
.B Constant Memory (O(1) RSS):
Input is processed in fixed 64\|KiB streaming buffers
.RB ( SNSLICE_IO_BUFFER_SIZE\ =\ 65536 ).
No heap allocations are performed for stream payloads or records.
Resident set size remains constant whether processing megabytes, gigabytes, or terabytes of data.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Record Length Safety Limit:
Individual records are bounded to a maximum of 16\|MiB
.RB ( SNSLICE_MAX_RECORD_BYTES\ =\ 16777216 ).
If a record exceeds 16\|MiB without reaching a valid record boundary,
.B snslice
aborts fail\-closed with diagnostic
.B snslice: RECORD_LENGTH_LIMIT: record exceeds 16 MiB limit
and exit status
.BR 2 .
This prevents memory exhaustion attacks from malformed or unbounded inputs.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Path Length Limits:
Constructed filesystem paths are bounded by
.B PATH_MAX
(4096 bytes).
Any path exceeding this limit fails closed with diagnostic
.B OUTPUT_DIR_ERROR
and exit status
.BR 2 .
.IP \(bu 3
.B Signal Handling:
.B SIGPIPE
is ignored unconditionally at startup via
.BR signal (2).
If an output pipe or downstream consumer closes prematurely (such as when piped to
.BR head (1)),
the condition surfaces as a checked stdio write error
.RB ( EPIPE )
triggering diagnostic
.B snslice: BROKEN_PIPE
and exit status
.BR 2 ,
with transactional cleanup of any partial chunk.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Diagnostic messages are printed strictly to standard error.
All error messages conform to the canonical format:
.RS 3
.P
.BI "snslice: " HAZARD_CODE ": " details
.RE
.P
Untrusted paths, filenames, and byte sequences rendered in diagnostics are sanitized:
characters outside printable ASCII (0x20 through 0x7E), double quotes
.RB ( \(dq ),
and backslashes
.RB ( \e )
are converted to uppercase hexadecimal sequences
.RB ( \ex HH ),
preventing ANSI terminal escape injection vulnerabilities.
.P
.B snslice
classifies all errors into a closed 16\-member hazard taxonomy:
.TP
.B USAGE_ERROR
Command\-line syntax error, arity violation, missing limits, invalid prefix, or combining informational flags.
.TP
.B UNKNOWN_OPTION
Unrecognized option flag passed on the command line.
.TP
.B INVALID_LIMIT
Argument for
.B \-\-bytes
or
.B \-\-records
is zero, negative, non\-numeric, or exceeds
.BR SIZE_MAX .
.TP
.B INVALID_FORMAT
Argument for
.B \-\-format
is neither
.B ndjson
nor
.BR csv .
.TP
.B INPUT_NOT_FOUND
Input file operand does not exist or lacks read permission.
.TP
.B INPUT_IS_DIRECTORY
Input file operand references a directory.
.TP
.B INPUT_READ_ERROR
Operating system read error encountered on the input stream.
.TP
.B RECORD_LENGTH_LIMIT
A single record exceeded the 16\|MiB limit without a delimiter.
.TP
.B MALFORMED_NDJSON
Unterminated line at EOF or embedded NUL byte detected in NDJSON input.
.TP
.B MALFORMED_CSV
Unterminated quoted field at EOF, bad quote escape, or embedded NUL byte in CSV input.
.TP
.B OUTPUT_DIR_ERROR
Output directory does not exist, is not a directory, or lacks write permissions.
.TP
.B OUTPUT_COLLISION
Target chunk file already exists on disk; aborting to prevent overwrite.
.TP
.B CHUNK_OPEN_ERROR
Operating system error creating a chunk file (other than collision).
.TP
.B CHUNK_WRITE_ERROR
Write or flush failure while writing data to a chunk file.
.TP
.B OUT_OF_MEMORY
Dynamic memory allocation failure.
.TP
.B BROKEN_PIPE
Output pipe closed prematurely while writing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success: input stream fully consumed, all complete records partitioned into valid chunk files,
and all files cleanly flushed and closed; or sole informational option
.RB ( \-\-help ,
.BR \-\-version )
executed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Reserved for notices or advisory findings in future versions.
.TP
.B 2
Operational or usage failure: command\-line syntax error, malformed input, limit violation,
file collision, directory error, I/O error, or broken pipe.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 3
.B Pure Partitioning:
.B snslice
partitions streams strictly by records; it does not validate JSON syntax within lines
or parse JSON values into an abstract syntax tree (AST).
.IP \(bu 3
.B No CSV Dialect Transformations:
.B snslice
preserves CSV input verbatim; it does not reorder columns, alter delimiters, or convert dialects.
.IP \(bu 3
.B No Integrated Compression:
.B snslice
does not decompress or compress streams directly.
Decompression and compression are delegated to standard Unix tools via pipelines
.RB ( "gzip \-dc input.gz | snslice ..." ).
.IP \(bu 3
.B No Cloud Staging or Network Operations:
.B snslice
performs no network communication, telemetry, socket operations, or cloud uploads.
.IP \(bu 3
.B No Background Services:
.B snslice
does not run background daemons, watch directories, or maintain persistent state across runs.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Single-Threaded Design:
.B snslice
runs as a single thread of execution, avoiding multithreading complexity, race conditions,
and synchronization overhead.
.SH EXAMPLES
Partition an NDJSON log stream into 10\|MiB chunks:
.P
.RS
.nf
snslice \-\-bytes 10485760 server.ndjson
.fi
.RE
.P
Partition an NDJSON stream into chunks of at most 50,000 records each:
.P
.RS
.nf
snslice \-\-records 50000 events.ndjson
.fi
.RE
.P
Partition a multiline CSV export from standard input with dual thresholds:
.P
.RS
.nf
cat database_export.csv | snslice \-\-format csv \-\-bytes 5242880 \-\-records 10000
.fi
.RE
.P
Specify a custom output directory and chunk filename prefix:
.P
.RS
.nf
snslice \-f ndjson \-b 1048576 \-d /var/data/chunks \-p part_ stream.ndjson
.fi
.RE
.P
Read from standard input explicitly using
.BR \- :
.P
.RS
.nf
snslice \-b 1000000 \- < input.ndjson
.fi
.RE
.P
Stream and slice a compressed dataset via external Unix pipeline:
.P
.RS
.nf
gzip \-dc large_export.csv.gz | snslice \-f csv \-b 50000000
.fi
.RE
.P
Display usage help:
.P
.RS
.nf
snslice \-\-help
.fi
.RE
.P
Display version information:
.P
.RS
.nf
snslice \-\-version
.fi
.RE
